latency-baseline.step_details:
## Where the 2-5 seconds actually goes

Traced end to end: `chat/[id]/+page.svelte:send()` → `POST /api/send` → `send_msg` →
`ensure` + `embed` + `upsert` → `relay` → `x2-ws` → `ChatHub` DO → `is_muted` → `total_unread` →
web push → response → *then* the bubble renders.

All timings below are **measured**, not estimated — against the live Qdrant cluster and the live
`api.voxell.ai` endpoint on 2026-07-30 via `node --env-file=.env`:

| operation | measured | where |
|---|---|---|
| `PUT /collections/x2/index` (no-op, index exists) | 157 ms | `qdrant.ts:103` |
| `GET /collections/x2` | 539 ms cold / ~150 ms warm | — |
| `POST /points/scroll` `s=m` limit 1000 (106 pts) | 424 ms | `unread.ts:39` |
| `PUT /points?wait=false` | 131 ms | `qdrant.ts:154` |
| `PUT /points?wait=true` | 449 ms | — |
| `PUT /points/vectors?wait=false` | 244 ms | — |
| `POST api.voxell.ai/v1/embeddings` `"hey"` | **1195 ms** | `or.ts:9` |
| same, `"how are you doing today"` | **852 ms** | — |
| same, `"ok"` | 660 ms | — |

### Budget for one 1:1 send, recipient offline

| # | stage | warm isolate | cold isolate |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | `ensure()` — 20 sequential no-op index creates | 0 ms | **3140 ms** |
| 2 | `embed()` — 4096-dim embedding, awaited before the write | **~900 ms** | ~900 ms |
| 3 | `upsert()` | 131 ms | 131 ms |
| 4 | `relay()` → `x2-ws` → `ChatHub` DO | ~50 ms | ~50 ms |
| 5 | `is_muted()` — 1 scroll | ~200 ms | ~200 ms |
| 6 | `total_unread()` — 3 parallel scrolls + `list_mutes`, sequential | ~620 ms | ~620 ms |
| 7 | `push()` — subs scroll + web-push POSTs | ~300 ms | ~300 ms |
| 8 | client renders the bubble — **only now** | | |
| | **total** | **~2.2 s** | **~5.3 s** |

That is the report exactly: ~2 s typically, up to ~5 s. The cold-isolate column is step 1 alone,
and Workers recycle isolates constantly, so it recurs — it is not a once-per-deploy cost.

Group sends are worse: stage 6 runs **once per offline member** (4 scrolls each), so a 16-member
room adds ~2 s more.

### Root causes, in order of size

1. **`ensure()` is 20 blind round trips per isolate** (`qdrant.ts:81-108`) against a collection
   that already has all 19 indexes. Pure waste. → step `ensure-one-rtt`
2. **The embedding API call is inside the write path** (`chat.ts:42` via `msg_vector`). ~900 ms
   on every message. → step `async-embedding`
3. **The sender awaits the recipient's push-notification pipeline** (stages 4-7). ~1.17 s that
   produces nothing in the response. → step `send-respond-early`
4. **The bubble does not render until the response arrives** (`+page.svelte:163`, after the
   `await fetch` on 151). Everything above is invisible to the user only once this is fixed. →
   step `optimistic-send-ui`

Storage and mechanism are untouched: Qdrant stays the only store, the `ChatHub` Durable Object
stays the live transport, web push stays the offline fallback. Nothing is cached, denormalised or
moved to another datastore. The only semantic change is that a message's *vector* becomes
eventually consistent (~1.2 s), which `backfill-vectors-script` backstops.

### Target

| | before | after |
|---|---|---|
| perceived (bubble on screen) | 2.2-5.3 s | **~0 ms** |
| sender server time, warm | ~1.85 s | **~180 ms** |
| sender server time, cold | ~5 s | **~330 ms** |
| recipient live delivery | 2.2-5.3 s | **~180 ms** |
| searchable by vector | at send | +~1.2 s |

## Implement

Land `scripts/latency-probe.mjs` so before/after is measurable rather than asserted. Same shape
as `scripts/purge-probe-users.mjs` — plain node, reads `.env`, no build step. Read-only against
Qdrant except for one probe point it writes and then deletes.

```js
// Times the individual operations on the send path. Read-only apart from a single probe point
// (fixed id, deleted at the end).
// Run:  node --env-file=.env scripts/latency-probe.mjs
const url = process.env.QDRANT_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const key = process.env.QDRANT_KEY;
const H = { 'api-key': key, 'content-type': 'application/json' };
const t = async (label, fn) => {
	const s = Date.now();
	const r = await fn();
	console.log(label.padEnd(34), `${Date.now() - s}ms`, r);
};

const info = await (await fetch(`${url}/collections/x2`, { headers: H })).json();
console.log('indexed keys:', Object.keys(info.result.payload_schema).sort().join(','));
console.log('points:', info.result.points_count);

await t('PUT index (no-op) x1', async () => {
	const r = await fetch(`${url}/collections/x2/index`, {
		method: 'PUT',
		headers: H,
		body: JSON.stringify({ field_name: 's', field_schema: 'keyword' })
	});
	return `${r.status}  -> ensure() does 20 of these`;
});

await t('scroll s=m limit1000', async () => {
	const r = await fetch(`${url}/collections/x2/points/scroll`, {
		method: 'POST',
		headers: H,
		body: JSON.stringify({
			filter: { must: [{ key: 's', match: { value: 'm' } }] },
			limit: 1000,
			with_payload: true,
			with_vector: false
		})
	});
	return `${r.status} n=${(await r.json()).result.points.length}`;
});

// fixed probe id, cleaned up below — never collides with a real UUID from crypto.randomUUID()
const ID = '00000000-0000-4000-8000-0000000000ff';
const ZV = new Array(4096).fill(0);
const P = { id: ID, vector: ZV, payload: { s: 'zzz_latency_probe', d: Date.now() } };
const pts = (qs, body, method = 'PUT') =>
	fetch(`${url}/collections/x2/points${qs}`, { method, headers: H, body: JSON.stringify(body) });

await t('upsert wait=false', async () => (await pts('?wait=false', { points: [P] })).status);
await t('upsert wait=true', async () => (await pts('?wait=true', { points: [P] })).status);
await t(
	'update_vectors wait=false',
	async () =>
		(await pts('/vectors?wait=false', { points: [{ id: ID, vector: ZV.map(() => 0.01) }] })).status
);
await t(
	'delete probe',
	async () => (await pts('/delete?wait=true', { points: [ID] }, 'POST')).status
);

const vk = process.env.VOXELL_KEY;
for (const s of ['hey', 'how are you doing today', 'ok']) {
	await t(`embed ${JSON.stringify(s.slice(0, 14))}`, async () => {
		const r = await fetch('https://api.voxell.ai/v1/embeddings', {
			method: 'POST',
			headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${vk}`, 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
			body: JSON.stringify({ model: 'jcorners/ingot-8b-r3', input: s })
		});
		const j = await r.json().catch(() => null);
		return `${r.status} dim=${j?.data?.[0]?.embedding?.length ?? 'n/a'}`;
	});
}
```

Latency from a dev machine is higher than from a Cloudflare colo, so these are *relative*
numbers — the ratios and the counts (20 index calls, 4 scrolls per member) are the point, not the
absolute milliseconds.

## Verify

The end-to-end acceptance check, after steps 1-7:

1. Open a chat in two browser profiles, DevTools Network open on the sender.
2. Type and hit send. **The bubble must be on screen in the same frame** — before any request
   completes. Confirm the `/api/send` request is still pending when the bubble is visible.
3. `/api/send` `Timing` → total under ~250 ms warm. Hard-reload first (fresh isolate) and
   confirm the first send is under ~400 ms, not ~5 s.
4. The recipient window shows the message within ~200 ms.
5. Send a PDF — recipient sees the file chip **live**, without reloading (step
   `relay-payload-fidelity`).
6. Wait 2 s, then search the thread for a distinctive word from the message and confirm it is
   found (the async vector landed).
7. Send from a room with an offline member and confirm the push still arrives.
8. `node --env-file=.env scripts/backfill-vectors.mjs` reports 0 stale points.

bg-task-helper.step_details:
## Why

Every latency fix below needs one primitive: "run this after the response is sent". On
Cloudflare Workers a promise that is not awaited and not registered with
`ExecutionContext.waitUntil()` is **killed** the moment the handler returns — so
fire-and-forget is not an option, the work would silently vanish.

`@sveltejs/adapter-cloudflare@7.2.8` already exposes the execution context. Verified in both
code paths:

- runtime worker: `node_modules/@sveltejs/adapter-cloudflare/files/worker.js:103-104` sets
  `platform = { env, cf, ctx, caches, context: ctx }`
- `vite dev` / preview proxy: `node_modules/@sveltejs/adapter-cloudflare/index.js:188-190`
  sets the same three keys off `getPlatformProxy()`

`src/app.d.ts:8` already declares `ctx: ExecutionContext` on `App.Platform`. Nothing reads it
yet. This step wires it to `locals` so endpoints never touch `platform` directly (they can't:
`platform.env` is a Proxy that **throws** on property access during prerender — see the
existing try/catch at `src/hooks.server.ts:23-30`).

## Implement

### 1. `src/app.d.ts` — extend `App.Locals`

```ts
interface Locals {
	user?: { id: string; username: string; picture?: string; email?: string } | null;
	x2_ws: Fetcher;
	geo: Geo | null;
	/**
	 * Keep a promise alive past the response. Backed by ExecutionContext.waitUntil in
	 * production; falls back to a floating promise (dev/test/prerender) where the isolate
	 * is not torn down at response time.
	 */
	bg: (p: Promise<unknown>) => void;
}
```

### 2. `src/hooks.server.ts` — populate it

Insert directly after the existing `event.locals.x2_ws = ...` line (currently line 31), reusing
the same defensive-try shape the file already uses for `platform.env` and `platform.cf`:

```ts
let wait_until: ((p: Promise<unknown>) => void) | undefined;
try {
	const ctx = event.platform?.ctx;
	if (ctx) wait_until = (p) => ctx.waitUntil(p);
} catch {
	wait_until = undefined;
}
// No ctx (prerender / vitest / non-CF adapter) => a floating promise is safe there; only
// workerd cancels un-registered promises at response time.
event.locals.bg = (p) => {
	const t = p.catch((e) => {
		console.error('[BG] background task failed', e);
	});
	if (wait_until) wait_until(t);
};
```

Two things are load-bearing:

- **the `.catch` wrapper.** An unhandled rejection inside `waitUntil` aborts the whole
  invocation context on workerd and can surface as a 500 *even after the response body was
  written* on some request shapes. Every background task must be pre-swallowed. This is also
  the only place a dropped background failure gets logged, so keep the `console.error` even
  though step 6 strips logging elsewhere — this one fires on failure only, not per message.
- **`wait_until` captured once per request**, not read from `platform` inside the closure —
  the closure may run after `event` is done and the Proxy may have been invalidated.

## Verify

`src/lib/__tests__/bg.test.ts` (new, one file, no framework beyond the vitest already in use):

```ts
import { describe, it, expect, vi } from 'vitest';
import { handle } from '../../hooks.server';

const run = async (platform: unknown) => {
	let captured: App.Locals['bg'] | undefined;
	const event = {
		cookies: { get: () => undefined, delete: () => {} },
		locals: {} as App.Locals,
		platform,
		url: new URL('https://x/')
	};
	await handle({
		event: event as never,
		resolve: async () => {
			captured = event.locals.bg;
			return new Response('ok');
		}
	} as never);
	return captured!;
};

describe('locals.bg', () => {
	it('registers the task with waitUntil when a ctx exists', async () => {
		const waitUntil = vi.fn();
		const bg = await run({ ctx: { waitUntil }, env: {}, cf: undefined });
		bg(Promise.resolve(1));
		expect(waitUntil).toHaveBeenCalledOnce();
	});

	it('swallows rejections so waitUntil never sees a rejected promise', async () => {
		const waitUntil = vi.fn();
		const bg = await run({ ctx: { waitUntil }, env: {}, cf: undefined });
		bg(Promise.reject(new Error('boom')));
		await expect(waitUntil.mock.calls[0][0]).resolves.toBeUndefined();
	});

	it('does not throw when there is no platform at all (prerender)', async () => {
		const bg = await run(undefined);
		expect(() => bg(Promise.resolve(1))).not.toThrow();
	});
});
```

`vi.mock('$env/dynamic/private', () => ({ env: {} }))` and a stub for `$lib/server/session`'s
`decode_session` are needed at the top, matching the pattern in
`src/routes/api/send/__tests__/server.test.ts:23`.

## Not doing

No queue, no Durable Object job runner, no retry/backoff. `waitUntil` is the platform
primitive for exactly this and it's already available. Add a real queue only if a background
task ever needs to outlive the 30s invocation budget or survive a colo failure — neither the
embedding backfill (~1.2s) nor the push fan-out (~1.5s) comes close.

ensure-one-rtt.step_details:
## The bug: `ensure()` costs ~3.1s on every cold isolate

`src/lib/server/qdrant.ts:81-108` runs, sequentially:

- 1 × `createCollection`
- 16 × `createPayloadIndex` (keyword: `s t r c f co st ci u ow mb gr uid ac tg k`)
- 1 × `createPayloadIndex` (`ag`, integer)
- 2 × `createPayloadIndex` (`at`, `sent`, integer)

= **20 sequential HTTP round trips to Qdrant, every one of them a guaranteed no-op in
production**, each `.catch(() => {})`-swallowed.

Measured against the live cluster (`node --env-file=.env`, 2026-07-30):

| call | latency |
|---|---|
| `PUT /collections/x2/index` (field `s`, already exists) | **157 ms** |
| `GET /collections/x2` (first call, incl. TCP+TLS) | 539 ms |
| `POST /points/scroll` `s=m` limit 1000 (106 pts) | 424 ms |
| `PUT /points?wait=false` (1 pt, 4096-dim) | **131 ms** |
| `PUT /points?wait=true` (1 pt, 4096-dim) | 449 ms |
| `PUT /points/vectors?wait=false` | 244 ms |

20 × 157 ms ≈ **3.14 s added to the first request each isolate serves.** This is the "sometimes
up to 5 seconds" in the report.

The live collection already has every index — `payload_schema` keys are:

```
ac ag at c ci co f gr j k mb ow r s sent st t tg u uid
```

That is all 19 that `ensure()` creates, plus a stale `j` from an earlier version. So the 3.14 s
buys literally nothing.

Two secondary defects in the same file:

1. **`ensured` is set only at the very end (line 107).** N concurrent requests arriving on a
   fresh isolate each run the whole 20-call sequence — a stampede, so a cold isolate under load
   is worse than 3.14 s, and 20N subrequests count against the 1000-subrequest cap.
2. **`qc(env)` (lines 26-32) resolves both secrets on *every* Qdrant operation.** With a
   `secrets_store_secrets` binding, `.get()` is an async RPC. A single `/api/send` calls `qc()`
   ~4× today, a group send with 15 offline members ~60×.

## Implement — `src/lib/server/qdrant.ts`

### 1. Cache the resolved credentials

```ts
let q: QdrantClient | null = null;
let q_key = '';
let creds: Promise<{ url: string; key: string }> | null = null;

export async function qc(env: QEnv): Promise<QdrantClient> {
	// Secrets Store .get() is an async RPC and every Qdrant op calls qc(); a secret cannot
	// change inside one isolate's lifetime, so resolve it once.
	creds ??= (async () => ({
		url: await get_secret(env.QDRANT_URL, env.DEV_QDRANT_URL),
		key: await get_secret(env.QDRANT_KEY, env.DEV_QDRANT_KEY)
	}))();
	const { url, key } = await creds;
	if (!q || q_key !== key) {
		q = new QdrantClient({ url, apiKey: key, checkCompatibility: false });
		q_key = key;
	}
	return q;
}

// test-only: drop the memoised client + creds between cases
export function __reset_qdrant(): void {
	q = null;
	q_key = '';
	creds = null;
	ensuring = null;
}
```

`__reset_qdrant` is needed because 8 existing test files import from `server/qdrant` and some
swap `$env/dynamic/private` between cases; without it a cached client leaks across them.
Call it in their `beforeEach`.

### 2. Probe-first, promise-cached, parallel `ensure()`

```ts
const KEYWORD_KEYS = [
	's', 't', 'r', 'c', 'f', 'co', 'st', 'ci', 'u', 'ow', 'mb', 'gr', 'uid', 'ac', 'tg', 'k'
] as const;
const INT_KEYS = ['ag', 'at', 'sent'] as const;

let ensuring: Promise<void> | null = null;

// One probe per isolate instead of 20 blind creates (measured: 3.14s -> ~150ms cold, ~0ms
// after). The promise (not a bool) is cached so concurrent first-requests share one probe
// rather than each running the full provision sequence.
export function ensure(env: QEnv): Promise<void> {
	return (ensuring ??= provision(env).catch(() => {
		ensuring = null; // never cache a failure — the next request retries
	}));
}

async function provision(env: QEnv): Promise<void> {
	const c = await qc(env);
	const need = [...KEYWORD_KEYS, ...INT_KEYS];
	try {
		const info = await c.getCollection(C);
		const have = new Set(Object.keys(info.payload_schema ?? {}));
		if (need.every((k) => have.has(k))) return;
	} catch {
		/* collection missing — fall through and create it */
	}
	await c.createCollection(C, { vectors: { size: 4096, distance: 'Cosine' } }).catch(() => {});
	await Promise.all([
		...KEYWORD_KEYS.map((k) =>
			c.createPayloadIndex(C, { field_name: k, field_schema: 'keyword' }).catch(() => {})
		),
		...INT_KEYS.map((k) =>
			c.createPayloadIndex(C, { field_name: k, field_schema: 'integer' }).catch(() => {})
		)
	]);
}
```

Deliberate details:

- **Check keys by name, not by count.** A count check (`>= 19`) would silently pass on the live
  collection the day someone adds a 20th filter key, because the stale `j` index pads the
  count — and `AGENTS.md` warns that filtering an unindexed key under `strict_mode` is
  *rejected outright*, so the query would just return nothing. Name-checking also makes
  `ensure()` self-healing: add a key to `KEYWORD_KEYS` and the next cold isolate creates it.
- **`ensure()` still never rejects**, matching today's contract — every caller does a bare
  `await ensure(env)` with no catch (e.g. `src/routes/app/chat/[id]/+page.server.ts:11`).
- **Creates are parallel now**, so even the genuine fresh-collection path is ~1 RTT instead
  of 20. `createCollection` stays sequential-before because the indexes need the collection.
- Do **not** delete the stale `j` index here. It costs nothing and dropping payload indexes
  from a request path is not something to do implicitly.

## Verify

`src/lib/server/__tests__/qdrant-ensure.test.ts` (new). Mock `@qdrant/js-client-rest` with a
fake client and assert:

1. `getCollection` returning all 19 keys ⇒ `createPayloadIndex` **never** called.
2. `getCollection` missing `tg` ⇒ `createCollection` called once and `createPayloadIndex`
   called 19 times.
3. `getCollection` rejecting ⇒ same as (2).
4. Two concurrent `ensure(env)` calls ⇒ `getCollection` called **once** (stampede guard).
5. `provision` rejecting ⇒ `ensure()` resolves (does not throw) **and** a subsequent
   `ensure()` retries (`getCollection` called twice).

## Expected result

Cold isolate: **3.14 s → ~150 ms.** Warm isolate: ~0 ms (awaiting a settled promise).
This alone removes the 5-second worst case.

send-respond-early.step_details:
## The bug: the sender waits for the recipient's push notification to be delivered

`src/routes/api/send/+server.ts`. After the message is stored, the handler awaits a chain that
the sender does not need and cannot see:

**1:1 path (lines 145-165):**

```
await relay(...)                       ~50ms   (service binding -> ChatHub DO)
await is_muted(env, to, me.id)         ~200ms  (1 Qdrant scroll)
await total_unread(env, to)            ~620ms  (unread_by_conv: 3 parallel scrolls @424ms
                                                + list_mutes: 1 scroll @200ms, sequential)
await push(...)                        ~300ms  (list_subs_many scroll + N web-push POSTs)
```

**≈1.17 s of pure post-delivery bookkeeping**, and it only runs when the recipient is *offline*
— i.e. the sender is punished precisely when there is no one waiting on the other end.

**Group path (lines 101-135) is worse:** `drop_muted` (a 1000-limit scroll) and then, for every
undelivered member, `total_unread(env, uid, [group_conv_id(group)])`. That is 4 Qdrant scrolls
*per offline member*. A 16-member room with 15 offline = 60 scrolls the sender waits on. The
`Promise.all` parallelises them but the handler still blocks on the slowest, and it burns
against the 1000-subrequest cap.

None of the four steps produce anything in the HTTP response. The response body
(lines 136-139, 166-169) is just `{ ok, m }`, built entirely from `m` — which exists before
any of this runs. `src/routes/app/chat/[id]/+page.svelte:158-170` and
`src/routes/app/rooms/[id]/+page.svelte:137-145` read only `body.m` / `body.scheduled`.

## Implement — `src/routes/api/send/+server.ts`

Keep `relay()` and `push()` exactly as they are. Extract each path's tail into a local async
function, and hand it to `locals.bg` (step 1) alongside the vector backfill (step 3).

### Group path

```ts
if (group) {
	const g = await get_group(env, group);
	if (!g) throw error(404, 'no group');
	if (!is_member(g, me.id)) throw error(403, 'not a member');
	const m = await send_group_msg(env, me.id, group, text, image, file);

	// fan out to every member but the sender, whose UI already appended it
	const fanout = async () => {
		const { undelivered } = await relay(
			{
				id: m.id,
				members: g.members.filter((u) => u !== me.id),
				group,
				from: me.id,
				from_name: me.username,
				text,
				image,
				file,
				ts: m.d
			},
			locals.x2_ws
		);
		const targets = await drop_muted(env, group, undelivered.filter((u) => u !== me.id));
		await Promise.all(
			targets.map(async (uid) => {
				const unread = await total_unread(env, uid, [group_conv_id(group)]);
				return push(env, [uid], {
					title: g.name,
					body: file ? `${me.username}: 📎 ${file.name}` : `${me.username}: ${text}`,
					url: `/app/rooms/${group}`,
					conv: group_conv_id(group),
					id: m.id,
					ts: m.d,
					kind: 'r',
					reply_to: group,
					unread,
					...(image ? { image: `/media/${image}` } : {})
				});
			})
		);
	};

	locals.bg(Promise.all([fanout(), backfill_vector(env, m.id, text)]));

	return json({
		ok: true,
		m: { id: m.id, from: m.f, group, text: m.x, image: m.im, file: m.fl, ts: m.d }
	});
}
```

Note the added `id: m.id` in the relay body — see step 5b, it fixes a live-delivery bug and is
needed here anyway.

### 1:1 path

```ts
if (!to) throw error(400, 'to or group required');
const m = await send_msg(env, me.id, to, text, image, file);

const fanout = async () => {
	const { undelivered } = await relay(
		{ id: m.id, to, from: me.id, from_name: me.username, text, image, file, ts: m.d },
		locals.x2_ws
	);
	if (!undelivered.includes(to)) return;
	if (await is_muted(env, to, me.id)) return;
	const unread = await total_unread(env, to);
	await push(env, [to], {
		title: me.username,
		body: file ? `📎 ${file.name}` : text,
		url: `/app/chat/${me.id}`,
		conv: conv_id(me.id, to),
		id: m.id,
		ts: m.d,
		kind: 'u',
		reply_to: me.id,
		unread,
		...(image ? { image: `/media/${image}` } : {})
	});
};

locals.bg(Promise.all([fanout(), backfill_vector(env, m.id, text)]));

return json({
	ok: true,
	m: { id: m.id, from: m.f, to: m.t, text: m.x, image: m.im, file: m.fl, ts: m.d }
});
```

`Promise.all` runs the fan-out and the embedding concurrently — the background window is
`max(~1.2s embed, ~1.5s fanout)`, not the sum.

### Also `src/routes/api/messages/[id]/edit/+server.ts`

The relay at line 39 is awaited for no reason (the response is `{ ok, m }`, built at line 45).
Wrap it: `locals.bg(locals.x2_ws.fetch(...).catch(() => {}))`. Same for the `backfill_vector`
call from step 3.

## Why the Qdrant upsert stays awaited

`await send_msg(...)` — 131 ms measured — remains in the critical path deliberately:

- it bounds the window in which a client reload could fail to see its own just-sent message
  (the insert is `wait=false`, so that window is already non-zero; do not widen it);
- it is the only thing the sender actually depends on;
- with step 5's optimistic UI the sender perceives 0 ms regardless, so there is nothing to buy.

Per `AGENTS.md`, `upsert()` swallows its own errors, so awaiting it does **not** give the sender
a failure signal. That is a known, pre-existing property and out of scope here — but it is the
reason not to bother restructuring further for a "did it store" guarantee.

## Deliberately not done: firing the relay before the upsert

Recipient-side latency after this step is `ensure(0) + upsert(131ms) + relay(~50ms) ≈ 180 ms`.
Issuing the relay first would make it ~50 ms, but it requires splitting `send_msg` into
`new_msg()` + `store_msg()`, touching both call sites in `scheduled.ts` and the test mocks.
180 ms is already below the perceptual threshold. Do it only if a room fan-out measurably
needs it.

## Verify — `src/routes/api/send/__tests__/server.test.ts`

31 existing cases, 28 assertion lines referencing `notifyMock` / `relay_body` /
`totalUnreadMock` / `isMutedMock` / `dropMutedMock`. All of that is now asynchronous, so the
`event()` helper must capture the background promise:

```ts
let bg_tasks: Promise<unknown>[] = [];

function event(body: unknown, uid: string | null = 'ada', fetcher = ws()) {
	return {
		request: new Request('https://x/api/send', { /* unchanged */ }),
		locals: {
			user: uid ? { id: uid, username: 'ada' } : null,
			x2_ws: fetcher,
			bg: (p: Promise<unknown>) => {
				bg_tasks.push(p);
			}
		}
	} as unknown as Parameters<typeof POST>[0];
}

// drain everything locals.bg received; call before asserting on relay/notify/unread
const settle = () => Promise.all(bg_tasks);
```

Add `bg_tasks = [];` to the existing `beforeEach` (next to `relay_body = undefined;`), and
insert `await settle();` after each `await POST(event(...))` in every case that asserts on the
relay body, `notifyMock`, `totalUnreadMock`, `isMutedMock` or `dropMutedMock`. Cases that only
assert the response body or a 4xx need no change.

Add three new cases:

1. **the response does not wait on the fan-out** — resolve `notifyMock` from a promise that is
   never settled, assert `POST(...)` still resolves with `{ ok: true, m: {...} }`.
2. **the fan-out is registered exactly once per send** — `bg_tasks.length === 1`.
3. **a throwing relay does not reject the handler** — `ws(new Error('x'))`, assert the response
   is 200 and, after `settle()`, that `notifyMock` was still called (the
   treat-as-undelivered fallback at `+server.ts:37-45` is preserved).

## Expected result

Server time for a 1:1 send: **~1.17 s → ~180 ms** when the recipient is offline (the common
case), unchanged-but-already-fast when online. Group send to a 16-member room:
**~2 s → ~180 ms.** Recipient live-delivery latency is unchanged (~180 ms) — the relay starts
at the same instant either way, it is only the *sender's* response that no longer waits on it.

optimistic-send-ui.step_details:
## The bug: the bubble does not exist until the server answers

This is the single largest contributor to *perceived* latency, and it is independent of every
server fix above.

`src/routes/app/chat/[id]/+page.svelte:118-174`:

- line 140 clears the input (`text = ''`) — so the composer empties immediately…
- …but `add_msg(...)` is only reached at line 163, **after** `await fetch` (151) **and**
  `await res.json()` (160).

So for the whole server round trip the user sees an empty input box and *no message*. Their text
has visibly vanished. Same shape in `src/routes/app/rooms/[id]/+page.svelte:116-146`
(`messages = [...]` at line 140, after the fetch).

Steps 2-4 cut the round trip to ~180 ms, which is already good, but a mobile RTT to the colo
plus TLS on a cold connection puts real-world perceived latency at 300-600 ms. Optimistic
render makes it **0 ms** and makes the send path resilient to a slow network instead of merely
faster on a fast one.

## Design: a stable client key, `cid`, that never changes

The `{#each}` blocks are keyed — `chat/[id]:409` and `rooms/[id]:372` both use `(m.id)`. Naively
appending with a temp id and then swapping in the server id **changes the key**, which makes
Svelte destroy and re-create the block: the bubble flickers and, when an image is attached, the
`<img>` re-mounts and re-fetches.

So: give optimistic messages a `cid` (a `crypto.randomUUID()`), key the each-block on
`m.cid ?? m.id`, and let `id` be filled in later. The key is stable from first paint; only `id`
mutates, and `id` is only read by edit/delete (which are disabled while pending anyway).

Rejected alternative: let the client generate the point id and send it as the message id. That
puts a client-controlled value into a Qdrant point id, and since `upsert` overwrites by id it
would let any authenticated user **overwrite any other user's message** by guessing an id. It
would need either server-side namespacing (`uuid_from('msg:' + uid + ':' + nonce)`, duplicating
the hash client-side) or a read-before-write RTT. Not worth it — `cid` gets the same
zero-flicker result with no trust boundary at all.

## Implement — `src/routes/app/chat/[id]/+page.svelte`

### 1. Widen the message type (line 32-34)

```ts
type Msg = {
	id: string;
	f: string;
	x: string;
	im?: string;
	fl?: FileAttach;
	d: number;
	cid?: string; // stable client key for an optimistically-rendered send
	err?: boolean; // the POST failed; offer a retry
};
let messages = $state(data.messages as Msg[]);
```

### 2. `add_msg` (line 41-53) — dedupe must ignore optimistic rows

```ts
function add_msg(m: Msg) {
	if (m.id && messages.some((e) => e.id === m.id)) return;
	const wasAtBottom = isAtBottom();
	messages = [...messages, m];
	if (wasAtBottom) tick().then(() => threadEl?.scrollTo({ top: threadEl.scrollHeight }));
}
```

The `m.id &&` guard matters: optimistic rows start with `id: ''`, and without it the second
pending message in a row would match the first on `'' === ''` and be dropped.

### 3. `send()` — rewrite lines 118-174

```ts
async function send(retry?: Msg) {
	const body = retry ? retry.x : text.trim();
	if ((!body && !pendingFile && !retry) || busy) return;

	let image = retry?.im;
	let file = retry?.fl;
	if (!retry && pendingFile) {
		busy = true; // the upload genuinely blocks: no key, nothing to render
		const r = await upload_file(pendingFile);
		busy = false;
		if (r.error || !r.key) return;
		// images stay inline-rendered (m.im); everything else renders as a download chip
		if (pendingFile.type.startsWith('image/')) image = r.key;
		else file = { key: r.key, name: r.name!, size: r.size!, type: r.type! };
		pendingFile = null;
	}

	const at = retry ? undefined : scheduleAt ? new Date(scheduleAt).getTime() : undefined;
	if (!retry) {
		text = '';
		scheduleAt = '';
		showSchedule = false;
	}

	// A scheduled send produces no message now, so there is nothing to render optimistically.
	let row: Msg | undefined;
	if (!at) {
		if (retry) {
			retry.err = false;
			row = retry;
		} else {
			row = { id: '', cid: crypto.randomUUID(), f: me, x: body, im: image, fl: file, d: Date.now() };
			add_msg(row);
		}
	}

	const res = await fetch('/api/send', {
		method: 'POST',
		headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json' },
		body: JSON.stringify({ to: data.peer, text: body, image, file, at })
	}).catch(() => null);

	if (!res?.ok) {
		if (row) row.err = true;
		return;
	}
	mark_first_send();
	const { m } = await res.json();
	if (row && m) {
		// only `id` changes — `cid` keeps the each-block key stable, so no re-mount
		row.id = m.id;
		row.d = m.ts;
	}
}
```

Notes:

- **`busy` no longer wraps the fetch.** It gated the composer for the whole round trip, so
  rapid-fire sending was impossible. It now covers only the upload, where blocking is real.
  The `if (... || busy) return` guard stays for the upload window.
- **`row.err = true` / `row.id = m.id` mutate the `$state` proxy in place.** Svelte 5's deep
  reactivity picks this up; the row object is reached through the `messages` state proxy, so
  the mutation is tracked. Do **not** rebuild the array here — that is what would churn keys.
- **`.catch(() => null)` on the fetch** — a hard network failure must land in the `err` branch,
  not throw out of the handler and leave the row pending forever. This is the data-loss path;
  it is the reason `err` exists at all rather than just silently retrying.
- `me` is already available: `let me = $derived($page.data.user?.id)` at line 78. Move that
  declaration above `send()` if the linter complains about use-before-declare (it is a
  `$derived`, so it is hoisted at runtime either way).

### 4. Render — line 409 onward

```svelte
{#each messages as m (m.cid ?? m.id)}
```

and on the bubble wrapper, a pending/failed affordance:

```svelte
class="... {m.id === '' && !m.err ? 'opacity-60' : ''}"
```

plus, after the bubble, inside the same column:

```svelte
{#if m.err}
	<button class="self-end text-[11px] text-[#e2674c] underline" onclick={() => send(m)}>
		not sent — retry
	</button>
{/if}
```

Guard the existing edit/delete controls with `m.id` so they cannot fire against an empty id
(grep for where `msg_id` is passed to `/api/messages/`).

## Implement — `src/routes/app/rooms/[id]/+page.svelte`

Same change, three differences:

1. `messages` is typed `Message[]` (line 29, from `$lib/types`). Use a local widened type
   rather than adding `cid`/`err` to the shared `Message` interface — they are view state, not
   stored fields: `type Row = Message & { cid?: string; err?: boolean }`.
2. Key at line 372 becomes `(m.cid ?? m.id)`.
3. **Add the missing dedupe to the ws handler** (line 200-216). It appends unconditionally,
   unlike `chat/[id]`'s `add_msg`. Today nothing dupes because the relay excludes the sender
   (`+server.ts:103`), but that is the only thing preventing it, and step 5b makes `m.id` real.
   Add before the append:

   ```ts
   if (messages.some((e) => e.id === m.id)) return;
   ```

   Also drop the `?? String(m.ts)` fallback on the id now that step 5b sends a real one.

The optimistic row for rooms needs the full shape:
`{ s: 'm', id: '', cid: crypto.randomUUID(), c: '', f: me!, t: '', gr: g.id, x: body, im: image, d: Date.now() }`
followed by `scroll_down()`.

## Not doing

No local object-URL preview for a pending image — the upload has to finish before there is a key
to send, so that window is unavoidable without a second rendering path. Add it if image sends on
slow uplinks feel bad.

## Verify

Extend `src/routes/app/chat/[id]/__tests__/` (note: `page.svelte.test.ts` there is currently
deleted in the working tree — restore or recreate it) with `@testing-library/svelte`:

1. **the bubble appears before the response** — resolve `fetch` from a promise you control;
   assert the text is in the DOM while it is still pending.
2. **no key churn on reconcile** — capture the bubble element before resolving the fetch,
   resolve it, assert `toBe` the *same* node afterwards. This is the flicker regression guard.
3. **failed send shows retry and keeps the text** — `fetch` rejects; assert
   `not sent — retry` is rendered and the message text is still on screen.
4. **retry re-POSTs and clears the error** — click retry with `fetch` now succeeding.
5. **a scheduled send (`at` set) renders no bubble.**
6. **two sends in a row both render** — the `m.id &&` dedupe guard.
7. rooms: **an incoming ws message with an id already present is ignored.**

## Expected result

Perceived send latency: **0 ms.** The bubble is on screen in the same frame as the keypress,
independent of network conditions, with an explicit failure state instead of a silently
swallowed message.

backfill-vectors-script.step_details:
## Why

Step 3 makes the message vector eventually consistent. The write path is
`upsert(ZV)` → *(response returns)* → `embed()` (~900 ms) → `update_vectors()`. Everything after
the response runs inside `waitUntil`, and there are real ways for it to not complete:

- `api.voxell.ai` errors or times out — `embed()` returns `ZV` and `backfill_vector` gives up
  silently (`or.ts:14`, `:18` swallow);
- `VOXELL_KEY` unset in an environment (`or.ts:7`);
- the isolate is evicted or the invocation hits its limit before `waitUntil` drains;
- `update_vectors` itself fails — `qdrant.ts` swallows every error, per `AGENTS.md`.

The failure is **silent and permanent**: the message is stored and displays fine, it just never
appears in `search_messages` results. Exactly the class of bug `AGENTS.md` warns about — "never
conclude 'no data' from an empty result".

There is also a pre-existing population to fix: messages sent before this change with text under
3 characters were deliberately stored with `ZV` by the old `msg_vector`, and any message sent
while `VOXELL_KEY` was unset likewise.

So: one offline reconciler, same shape as the existing `scripts/purge-probe-users.mjs`.

## Implement

New file `scripts/backfill-vectors.mjs`. Plain node, no build step, reads creds from `.env` the
way the existing script does. `scripts/` already exists.

```js
// Finds s:'m' points whose vector is all-zero and patches in a real embedding.
// Safety net for the async backfill in src/lib/server/chat.ts (embedding API down, isolate
// evicted mid-waitUntil), and a one-time fix for messages stored before that change.
//
// Run:  node --env-file=.env scripts/backfill-vectors.mjs [--apply] [--limit=500]
const url = process.env.QDRANT_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const key = process.env.QDRANT_KEY;
const vkey = process.env.VOXELL_KEY;
const apply = process.argv.includes('--apply');
const limit = Number(process.argv.find((a) => a.startsWith('--limit='))?.slice(8) ?? 500);
const MODEL = 'jcorners/ingot-8b-r3'; // keep in sync with src/lib/server/or.ts

const H = { 'api-key': key, 'content-type': 'application/json' };
const q = (path, body, method = 'POST') =>
	fetch(`${url}/collections/x2${path}`, { method, headers: H, body: JSON.stringify(body) }).then(
		async (r) => {
			if (!r.ok) throw new Error(`${r.status} ${await r.text()}`);
			return r.json();
		}
	);

const embed = async (text) => {
	const r = await fetch('https://api.voxell.ai/v1/embeddings', {
		method: 'POST',
		headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${vkey}`, 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
		body: JSON.stringify({ model: MODEL, input: text.slice(0, 8000) })
	});
	if (!r.ok) throw new Error(`embed ${r.status} ${await r.text()}`);
	return (await r.json()).data[0].embedding;
};

// Paged scroll with_vector:true — 4096 floats/point is ~90KB of JSON, so keep pages small.
let offset = null;
const stale = [];
do {
	const { result } = await q('/points/scroll', {
		filter: { must: [{ key: 's', match: { value: 'm' } }] },
		limit: 64,
		offset,
		with_payload: true,
		with_vector: true
	});
	for (const p of result.points) {
		const text = (p.payload.x ?? '').trim();
		if (text.length < 3) continue; // intentionally ZV — embed() would return ZV anyway
		if (Array.isArray(p.vector) && p.vector.some((v) => v !== 0)) continue;
		stale.push({ id: p.id, text });
	}
	offset = result.next_page_offset;
} while (offset && stale.length < limit);

console.log(`${stale.length} message(s) with a zero vector and >=3 chars of text`);
for (const { id, text } of stale) console.log(`  ${id}  ${JSON.stringify(text.slice(0, 60))}`);
if (!apply) {
	console.log('\ndry run — pass --apply to patch');
	process.exit(0);
}

let ok = 0;
for (const { id, text } of stale) {
	try {
		const vector = await embed(text);
		// update_vectors, not upsert: never rewrite the payload from this script, it would
		// clobber a concurrent edit.
		await q('/points/vectors?wait=true', { points: [{ id, vector }] }, 'PUT');
		ok++;
	} catch (e) {
		console.error(`  FAILED ${id}: ${e.message}`);
	}
}
console.log(`patched ${ok}/${stale.length}`);
```

Deliberate details:

- **`--apply` gated, dry-run by default**, matching `scripts/purge-probe-users.mjs`. This writes
  to the live collection; never make that the default.
- **`update_vectors`, not `upsert`.** Same reasoning as step 3 — the script reads a payload and
  could be racing a live edit, and re-upserting the payload it read would revert that edit.
- **`wait=true` here**, unlike the request path. Throughput does not matter offline and it makes
  the reported count honest.
- **Sequential embed loop, not `Promise.all`.** ~900 ms each; on the current 106 messages that is
  ~95 s worst case, which is fine, and it avoids rate-limiting the embedding API.
  `ponytail: serial embeds, ~1s/message — batch or bound the concurrency if this ever has to
  chew through 10k messages.`
- **`vector.some((v) => v !== 0)`** rather than a checksum: 4096 floats, an early exit on the
  first non-zero, negligible.
- Uses the **plain REST API**, not `@qdrant/js-client-rest`, so it runs as a bare `.mjs` with no
  bundler — same choice the existing script makes.

## Verify

Run it against the live collection dry-run first:

```
node --env-file=.env scripts/backfill-vectors.mjs
```

Expected on the current data: it lists whatever subset of the 106 `s:'m'` points has a zero
vector (at minimum, any short-text messages that slipped past the old 3-char check, plus
anything sent during a `VOXELL_KEY` outage), and writes nothing. Then `--apply`, then re-run the
dry run and confirm it reports 0.

A cheap end-to-end check of step 3 while in here: send a message, immediately run the dry run
(it should list the new id), wait ~2 s, run again (it should not).

## Not doing

No cron trigger for this. The `waitUntil` path is expected to work; this is a reconciler you
reach for when search looks wrong, not a scheduled job. Wire it into
`ws/src/index.ts`'s `scheduled` handler only if the dry run keeps finding rows.
